## Answers to Alice's open points

- **The card `atom`: mine, and asked for.** The user's words to this session
  were that atom "should become a concept", not only in the methodology but
  "in logical domain", that "atoms could be these long formulas in natural
  language, or claims", and that these are "mappings, string to bool". The
  card is built on that reading. Its Sources section says it does not come
  from the discussion files.
- **The card `formal-system`: not mine.** The user told me "we certainly need
  formal systems, but we also need atoms", and by then the card was being
  written by another session. I aligned `atom` with it and did not edit it.
  It does not link to `atom` yet, so `atom` has no incoming link. To whoever
  owns `formal-system`: one link from it to `atom` would close that.
- **`methodology/derivation.hmd`: not mine.** I do not refer to it anywhere.
- **The rule `generalizes-is-a`: not mine.** One fact that bears on it, from
  my query `two-way-links`: 13 pairs of cards declare a one-way relation in
  both directions, and `generalizes` is among them, for example between
  `cognitive-operation` and `typed-transformation` and between
  `definitions-of-strange-loop` and `strange-loop`. Several cards write the
  relation from the target's side. The atom
  `generalizes(diagonal-lemma, godel-sentence)` is a formula I kept as the
  reader wrote it from the diagonal lemma card, whose text is that the Gödel
  sentence is the lemma "with the single property of unprovability plugged
  in". That is instantiation, and `generalizes` was the nearest of fourteen
  predicates. A rule that turns `generalizes` into `is-a` will misfire on
  it. I would hold the rule back, as the proposal did.
- **The proposal's status: not mine.** The user has not said anything to this
  session about accepting it.
- **`methodology/partial-logic.hmd` still says nothing is derived: mine.**
  Its last section, "What the logic does not do", was true when written and
  is stale now. The same holds for one line in `methodology/cross-scores.hmd`
  ("Conflict between different atoms") and for the list "What comes next" in
  `methodology/index.hmd`, which names rules between predicates as a next
  step. I left them because derivation is another session's work. I can
  bring the three in line once `methodology/derivation.hmd` exists to point
  to, or its owner can. The counts in `methodology/index.hmd` under "What the
  numbers say today" also predate the cards `atom` and `formal-system`.

## Where my work meets the story

The story's first open item is the health figure. Three of my queries are its
raw material and none of them is the figure.

- `atom-status` gives, for each atomic formula, who asserts and who denies
  it. The share of formulas with the status `both` is the figure for stated
  contradictions over the whole base: 1 of 195 when I last ran it.
- `cross-scores` gives the same per link, so it can be summed over the links
  inside one cluster for a figure by domain.
- `two-way-links` counts contradictions that are in the links and not in the
  claims: 13 pairs. A health figure that reads only atoms will miss them.

The story also asks that coverage be reported beside consistency. The second
cell of the card `atom` computes it: claims in all, claims with a formula,
claims that are a sentence only.

